<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tynwald</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tynwald Court</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tynwald is the parliament of the Isle of Man, which descends from the Norse assembly, or thing, of the Kingdom of Mann and the Isles and claims a continuous existence of more than a thousand years. Each year on 5 July it holds an open-air sitting at Tynwald Hill in St John's, where new laws are proclaimed in English and Manx.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tynwald.jpg" alt="Tynwald" width="320" height="165" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Junior Jumper · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Legislative_Council_of_the_Isle_of_Man_as_of_Apr_2020.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Tradition dates Tynwald to 979, and the Manx people marked its millennium in 1979, but the year was picked arbitrarily by officials and no evidence shows that such an assembly met then. The available historical evidence points to the 13th century. The first record of the place-name occurs in the Chronicles of Mann of the 13th and 14th centuries, and the first description of the role and composition of an assembly held on the site dates from the early 15th century. The name derives from the Old Norse þingvǫllr, meeting place of the thing, or assembly, as in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iceland">Iceland</a>'s Þingvellir.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Tynwald originally comprised only the 24 members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-keys">House of Keys</a>, four for each of the six sheadings of the island. The earliest surviving record of the Keys dates from 1417. Membership was for life, the remaining members chose any replacement, and seats generally passed down through leading families. In 1600 the Keys became a permanent body, and the power to make new laws developed from their role of declaring the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/common-law">common law</a> around 1610.</p><p>The island fell to Parliamentary forces in October 1651, and Tynwald met only sporadically. After the Restoration the Lords of Mann returned and the Keys lost power. In 1737 Tynwald's agreement became required for all taxation, but in 1765 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-of-mann">Lord of Mann</a> sold his rights to the British Crown, and Tynwald was left with little power and no money. A Royal Commission was appointed in 1791, after the Keys petitioned for a new elected parliament. In 1866 legislation provided for some members to be elected for the first time. Marion Shimmin was the first woman elected, at a by-election in 1933.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Tynwald has two branches, the directly elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-keys">House of Keys</a> and the indirectly chosen Legislative Council, which together sit as Tynwald Court. The Keys originate most legislation and the Council acts as a revising chamber. If the Council rejects a bill against the Keys' wishes, the Keys may repass it without the Council's approval.</p><p>On Tynwald Day, usually 5 July, the court meets in the open air at Tynwald Hill at St John's, presided over by the Lieutenant Governor unless the monarch as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-of-mann">Lord of Mann</a>, or a representative of the Royal Family, is present. All laws are promulgated there in both Manx and English and petitions are received. An Act not promulgated at St John's within 18 months of passage becomes void.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tynwald” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Man kept its own <b>Tynwald</b>, with the elected House of Keys and the Legislative Council, its own laws and taxes.</blockquote><a href="/state/isle-of-man">Isle of Man</a></li><li><blockquote>Man's <b>Tynwald</b> assembly descends from its Norse institutions.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-isles">Kingdom of the Isles</a></li><li><blockquote>The island kept the <b>Tynwald</b>, its own laws and the Manx language.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-mann">Lordship of Mann</a></li></ul></section>
